What is K-Means?

K-Means is an unsupervised machine learning algorithm used to divide data into a specified number of groups called clusters.

For customer segmentation, customers having similar characteristics are placed into the same cluster.

For example:

              Customers
                  ↓
             K-Means
                  ↓
       ┌──────────┼──────────┐
       ↓          ↓          ↓
    Cluster 1  Cluster 2  Cluster 3
       ↓          ↓          ↓
    Similar    Similar    Similar
   Customers  Customers  Customers

Customer segmentation helps businesses create targeted marketing campaigns, improve engagement, and provide better recommendations.

How K-Means Works

Suppose we choose:

$$ K = 3 $$
Step 1 — Choose K

Select the number of clusters.

Example:

K = 3
Step 2 — Initialize centroids

K-Means initially chooses 3 centroid points.

Step 3 — Assign data points

Each customer is assigned to the nearest centroid, usually using Euclidean distance.

Step 4 — Recalculate centroids

The center of each cluster is calculated again based on its assigned customers.

Step 5 — Repeat

Customers are reassigned and centroids are recalculated until the assignments stabilize or the algorithm reaches its stopping condition.

Simple flow
Choose K
   ↓
Initialize Centroids
   ↓
Assign Customers
   ↓
Calculate New Centroids
   ↓
Repeat
   ↓
Final Customer Clusters
