Q8. What are the limitations of the K-Means algorithm?

Important limitations include:

1. Need to select K

The number of clusters must be chosen beforehand.

2. Sensitive to initialization

Different initial centroids can sometimes produce different results.

3. Sensitive to outliers

Extreme data points can affect cluster centroids.

4. Sensitive to feature scaling

Features with larger scales can dominate distance calculations.

5. Assumes cluster structure suitable for centroid-based grouping

K-Means works best when clusters are reasonably compact and separable according to the distance measure.

6. Not ideal for every data type

Standard K-Means is designed for numerical features and distance-based grouping.

Q9. How can businesses use customer segmentation to improve marketing and customer retention?

Businesses can divide customers into groups based on:

Income
Age
Spending behavior
Purchase frequency
Purchasing patterns

Then they can create strategies for each group.

Example:
Customer Group	Possible Strategy
High income + high spending	Premium offers
High income + low spending	Personalized promotions
Low income + high spending	Discounts and loyalty rewards
Low income + low spending	Cost-effective offers

This allows companies to avoid treating every customer in exactly the same way.

As a result, segmentation can support targeted marketing, better customer engagement, personalized recommendations, and retention strategies.

